course: dictionary
lesson: 4154
title: Words from science and craft
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are only used in certain lessons, like science or craft. You can still work them out from the words around them. «Soap makes the water's skin flexible so it does not snap, which is why plain water cannot be blown into bubbles; a little glycerine or sugar makes bubbles last longer.» The sentence is about bubbles and soap, so glycerine must be something that helps bubbles last longer.
explain: Look for clues in the sentence. Here the writer says that plain water cannot be blown into bubbles, but glycerine or sugar can help. So glycerine is something you add to water to make bubbles stronger. Always check your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of the hard word.
explain: Sometimes a word has two spellings. Glycerine and glycerin are the same thing, and glycerin is the American spelling. In science, the same thing has a more technical name, glycerol. Scientists use the longer name in lab work, but you will see glycerine on labels and in craft kits.
fact: Glycerine is a thick, clear liquid with no colour and no smell.
fact: Glycerine tastes sweet, and it is used in soap, medicines, cosmetics and food.
fact: On food labels, glycerine is often written as E422.
fact: In science, glycerine is the everyday name for glycerol, a chemical made from fats and oils.
fact: «Glycerine» is a noun, and it is uncountable, so you say «some glycerine», not «a glycerine».
exercise: «A little glycerine makes bubbles last longer.» What does glycerine help bubbles do? | *Last longer | Pop straight away | Sink to the bottom | Turn a different colour
exercise: Nadia reads that glycerine is a clear liquid with no smell. What is glycerine? | *A thick, clear, sweet-tasting liquid | A hard, grey metal | A gas you can see | A powder that dissolves in fire
exercise: Find the word on a label that means glycerine in food. | flour | *E422 | water | sugar
test: «Soap makes the water's skin flexible so it does not snap.» What does flexible mean here? | Frozen | *Bendy and stretchy | Hard and stiff | Coloured
test: Ella's craft kit says glycerin. What is the best way to explain this to a friend? | *It is another spelling of glycerine | It is a different thing from glycerine | It is a type of bubble | It is the name of a colour
exam: Jayden reads, «Plain water cannot be blown into bubbles, but a little glycerine can.» Using the clues, what can you say about glycerine? | *It helps water make bubbles that last | It has a strong smell and a bright colour | It is a sort of soap that is hard to blow | It stops bubbles from forming, so it is not useful
